    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Yes, you can use nonstick pans on induction, but only if the pan has a magnetic steel base; most nonstick is aluminum, and plain aluminum will not heat. The real catch is speed: induction can push an empty nonstick pan past the roughly 500 degree Fahrenheit range where PTFE coatings start to break down far faster than gas or radiant electric. Use a pan with an induction base, never preheat it empty on high, and keep everyday nonstick cooking at low to medium settings.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Will a Nonstick Pan Work on an Induction Hob?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nonstick is a coating, not a metal, so the answer depends on what is underneath. The vast majority of nonstick pans have aluminum bodies because aluminum is light, cheap and spreads heat well. Aluminum is not magnetic, so a plain aluminum nonstick pan will not be detected by an induction hob. Pans sold as induction compatible have a magnetic stainless plate bonded to the base or a clad stainless exterior, and those work well.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the first test is the same as for any pan: hold a fridge magnet to the center of the base. A firm grip means the pan will heat. No grip means it will not, regardless of how new or expensive the coating is. Our explainer on <a href="/can-you-use-aluminum-on-induction/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">aluminum on induction</a> covers the different base types and how each one performs.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Nonstick on Induction at a Glance</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Base</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Must be magnetic</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~500°F</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Where PTFE starts to suffer</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Low-Med</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Everyday settings</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Never</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Preheat empty on high</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Once a nonstick pan is compatible, it is a good match for induction in many ways. Eggs, pancakes, fish and anything delicate benefit from the precise, repeatable low settings induction offers. The coating does not care what makes the heat. What changes is how quickly the heat arrives, and that is where most of the problems people have with nonstick on induction come from.
          </p>

          <p>
            That speed is why this page spends more time on temperature than on compatibility. An induction-ready nonstick pan used on medium will last as long as on any other cooktop. The same pan routinely preheated empty on boost can be ruined in weeks, and in the worst case can give off fumes you do not want in the kitchen.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="base" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How Do You Know If Your Nonstick Pan Has an Induction Base?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Turn the pan over. An induction-ready nonstick pan usually shows a circle of bright, polished stainless on an otherwise dull or dark base, or a stainless disc with a pattern of holes pressed into the aluminum. Many have the induction symbol, a coil or zigzag, stamped into the plate. A uniform dark or matte base with no plate is almost always plain aluminum.
          </p>

          <p>
            Confirm with a magnet in the center of the base. Then try the pan on the hob with a splash of water rather than empty, so you are not testing compatibility by overheating the coating. If it heats within seconds, you are set. If the hob flashes an error, check that the pan is centered and big enough for the zone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to how much of the base is covered. A full plate heats the whole pan evenly. A smaller pressed disc heats the center well and leaves the outer ring cooler, which shows up as pancakes that brown in the middle and stay pale at the edges. That is not a fault, just a limitation of the cheaper construction. Matching the pan to a zone the size of the disc helps.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hybrid pans with a stainless mesh or pattern over a nonstick surface usually sit on a magnetic tri-ply body and work very well on induction. Clad stainless pans with a nonstick interior are the same. If you are shopping, our <a href="/best-induction-cookware/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">best induction cookware</a> guide lists nonstick options with the base type spelled out.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="overheating" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Is Overheating Nonstick Easier on Induction?
          </h2>

          <p>
            On gas or radiant electric, heat has to travel from a flame or a glowing element into the pan, and a lot of it escapes along the way. Induction makes heat inside the base of the pan itself, with very little loss. A thin aluminum nonstick pan has very little mass to absorb that energy, so with nothing in it the temperature climbs extremely quickly, especially on the highest settings or a boost function.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most cooks build their habits on slower cooktops. Putting a pan on high and walking away for a couple of minutes to prep ingredients is normal on gas. On induction that same habit can take an empty nonstick pan well past the temperature the coating is designed for before you are back at the stove. The pan gives little warning, and you will not see a flame or glowing element to remind you it is on full.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Never preheat an empty nonstick pan on high or boost.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Add oil or food first, start on a low to medium setting, and only increase power once there is something in the pan to absorb the heat.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Fat helps you here. A thin film of oil in the pan acts as a built-in thermometer: it shimmers as it gets hot and starts to smoke well before the coating reaches dangerous temperatures for most cooking oils. If oil is smoking in a nonstick pan, the pan is too hot. Turn it down, and on induction the pan responds within seconds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some induction hobs include a frying sensor or temperature-controlled zone that holds the pan at a set temperature rather than a power level. If yours has one, it is ideal for nonstick, because it stops the pan from running away. Check whether it needs a specific pan type to work, since some sensors are designed for particular cookware.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="temperatures" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Hot Is Too Hot for Nonstick?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Traditional nonstick uses PTFE, the coating widely known by the Teflon brand name. It is stable at normal cooking temperatures. Above roughly 500 degrees Fahrenheit, about 260 Celsius, PTFE starts to degrade, and at higher temperatures it can release fumes that cause flu-like symptoms in people, sometimes called polymer fume fever. Pet birds are especially sensitive to these fumes, which is why bird owners are routinely told to be cautious with nonstick.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Task</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Pan Temperature</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Nonstick OK?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Eggs, pancakes, fish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">250 to 375°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes, ideal</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sauteing vegetables</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">325 to 400°F</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hard searing steak</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">450°F and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Use cast iron or stainless instead</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Empty pan on high or boost</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Can exceed 500°F quickly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            In normal use you rarely get near those numbers, because food and liquid keep the pan cooler. Water cannot get much past its boiling point, and most foods release moisture that holds the pan down. The danger comes from empty or nearly empty pans on high settings, which is exactly the situation induction makes easy to stumble into.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most cookware makers tell you to use nonstick on low to medium heat and not to preheat it empty. Read the instructions that came with your pan, because some coatings and pans have specific oven-safe and stovetop limits. If a manufacturer says medium heat maximum, believe them; their warranty usually depends on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For high-heat jobs, use a different pan. Searing steak, blackening fish and stir-frying at full power belong in cast iron, carbon steel or clad stainless, all of which tolerate high temperatures without a coating to worry about. Our page on <a href="/does-cast-iron-work-on-induction/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">cast iron on induction</a> covers how to use it without stressing the glass.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ceramic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Ceramic Nonstick Better for Induction?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ceramic nonstick is a silica-based, sol-gel coating applied to a metal pan, usually aluminum. It contains no PTFE, so the fume concern above does not apply in the same way, and many ceramic coatings tolerate higher temperatures before they break down. Like PTFE pans, ceramic pans only work on induction if they have a magnetic base; the word ceramic refers to the coating, not the pan body.
          </p>

          <p>
            The trade-off is lifespan. Ceramic coatings tend to lose their slickness faster than good PTFE, and high heat speeds that up. Food starts to stick, often within a year or two of regular use, even when the coating looks intact. Many ceramic pan makers still recommend low to medium heat for exactly that reason, so the higher temperature tolerance is less useful than it sounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            On induction, ceramic and PTFE pans behave the same way: fast heat-up, quick response and the same need to avoid empty high-power preheating. If you prefer to avoid PTFE altogether, ceramic is a reasonable choice. Just expect to replace it more often, and treat it gently to get the most from it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want something PTFE-free that lasts longer, well-seasoned carbon steel or cast iron becomes reasonably nonstick with use and handles high heat without complaint. They are heavier and need care, but they will outlast any coated pan. A sensible induction kitchen often has one coated pan for eggs and one bare metal pan for everything else.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="settings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Settings Should You Use for Nonstick on Induction?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Power levels differ by brand, so treat these as starting points rather than rules. On a hob with settings from 1 to 9 or 1 to 10, eggs and pancakes usually do well around the lower-middle settings, sauteing in the middle range, and very little nonstick cooking needs the top settings. Boost should be reserved for boiling water in a pot, not for nonstick skillets.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add oil or butter first, then turn the zone on. Give the pan thirty seconds to a minute on a moderate setting and watch the fat: butter foaming or oil shimmering means you are ready. If you prefer to preheat with nothing in the pan, keep it brief and moderate, and never on the highest settings. Then adjust once food is in, which on induction takes effect almost immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            Center the pan on the zone and match the size. A small pan on a large zone may not be detected, and a pan with a small induction disc heats best on a zone sized to that disc. Lift rather than slide to protect both the glass and the pan base. Some light buzzing at higher settings is normal; our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-induction-cooktop-buzzing/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">why induction cooktops buzz</a> explains the sounds.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not walk away from a nonstick pan on induction during preheat. It is the single most common way coatings get damaged. Once food is in and the setting is moderate, the pan is far more forgiving, but the first minute is where the habit matters most.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lifespan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. How Long Does Nonstick Last on Induction, and When Should You Replace It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Induction itself does not wear out nonstick faster. Overheating, metal utensils, abrasive scrubbers and dishwashers do. With reasonable care, a good PTFE pan typically lasts a few years of regular use and ceramic coatings often less. Used gently on low to medium settings, an induction-ready nonstick pan should last as long as it would on any cooktop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replace a nonstick pan when food sticks even with fat, when the coating is visibly flaking or deeply scratched, or when the base has warped so the pan rocks on the glass. Warping is worth watching on induction, because a rocking base heats unevenly, can buzz and may not be detected reliably. Thin, cheap aluminum pans warp most easily.
          </p>

          <p>
            To get the most from a pan, use wooden or silicone tools, let the pan cool before washing, and hand-wash if the maker recommends it. Store pans with a protector or cloth between them so they do not scratch each other. Avoid cooking sprays that leave a gummy residue; a little oil or butter works better and is easier on the coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you replace, buy a pan with a full-coverage magnetic base rather than a small pressed disc, and in a size that matches the zone you use most. If you are also wondering about the hob itself, our page on <a href="/is-induction-cooking-safe/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">whether induction cooking is safe</a> covers the broader safety questions.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Preheating empty on boost</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Boost and the top settings can take a thin, empty nonstick pan past the temperature the coating is designed for very quickly. That damages the surface and, with PTFE, can produce fumes. Put fat or food in the pan first, start at a moderate setting, and save boost for pots of water.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every nonstick pan is induction ready</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most nonstick pans are aluminum, and aluminum does not work on induction without a magnetic plate. Check the base for a stainless circle or the coil symbol and test with a magnet in the center. A pan that will not hold a magnet will not heat, however new it is.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Searing in nonstick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hard searing needs pan temperatures well above what most nonstick makers recommend. Doing it regularly shortens the coating's life and risks overheating. Keep a cast iron, carbon steel or clad stainless pan for steaks and high-heat stir-fries, and reserve nonstick for eggs, fish and gentler cooking.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Walking away during preheat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On induction the first minute is when an empty or nearly empty pan heats fastest. Stepping away to chop or answer the door is how coatings get scorched. Stay with the pan until fat is shimmering or food is in, then adjust the setting and relax.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring a warped base</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A thin nonstick pan that has warped rocks on the glass, heats unevenly, can buzz and may not be detected properly. Check by setting it on a flat counter. Once the base is bowed, it rarely recovers, and replacing it is better than fighting it every morning.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is Teflon safe on an induction cooktop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                PTFE nonstick is considered safe at normal cooking temperatures on any cooktop, including induction. The concern is overheating, which induction makes easier with empty pans on high settings. Keep nonstick to low and medium settings, never preheat it empty on boost, and ventilate the kitchen, particularly if you keep pet birds.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does induction ruin nonstick pans faster?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not by itself. Induction heats the pan quickly but does not damage coatings directly. Overheating, metal utensils, abrasive cleaners and dishwashers are what wear nonstick out. Used on moderate settings with gentle tools, an induction-ready nonstick pan should last about as long as it would on gas or radiant electric.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What setting is medium on an induction cooktop?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies by brand and by the number of steps. On a 1 to 9 or 1 to 10 scale, medium is usually around the middle, roughly 4 to 6. Because induction is so responsive, it is worth testing with a pan of water or butter to learn where your hob's settings actually land.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can nonstick pans scratch the induction glass?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Nonstick coatings themselves are soft, but the steel plate on the base can scratch glass if it is dragged across grit or salt. Lift pans rather than sliding them, wipe the glass before cooking and keep the base clean. Most visible marks on glass are residue that a ceramic cooktop cleaner removes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are PFOA-free pans different on induction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. PFOA was a chemical used in making some older PTFE coatings and has been phased out of US nonstick manufacturing. Whether a pan is labeled PFOA-free has no effect on induction compatibility. That depends only on whether the base is magnetic. The same temperature guidance applies to all PTFE pans.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does my nonstick pan brown only in the middle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That usually means the pan has a small pressed-in steel disc, and only the area above the disc heats strongly, or the pan is bigger than the zone. Use a zone that matches the disc or pan size, preheat a little longer at moderate power, and consider a full-plate base when replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I use cooking spray on nonstick on induction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most nonstick makers advise against aerosol sprays, because the additives can build up into a sticky residue that reduces release and is hard to clean. A small amount of oil or butter works better. The cooktop type does not change this advice, but residue can burn on faster on induction's quick heat.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

