        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Nonstick works on induction when the base is magnetic, and a fridge magnet tells you in seconds. The part that takes adjustment is heat. Induction is fast enough that an empty nonstick pan can blow past its safe range before you notice, so the habits that were harmless on gas need changing.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Put fat or food in first, cook on low to medium, keep boost for pots of water, and use a bare metal pan for searing. Do that and an induction-ready nonstick pan will be one of the easiest, most precise tools in your kitchen.
          </p>
